      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">Profit and Loss Appropriation Account</span> shows how the net profit (or loss) of a partnership business is shared among the partners, after all trading expenses have already been deducted.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning and Purpose</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">While a sole trader keeps the whole profit, a partnership must divide profit fairly among two or more partners according to their partnership agreement. The Profit and Loss Appropriation Account is prepared immediately after the ordinary Profit and Loss Account, starting with the net profit brought down, and shows exactly how that profit is distributed.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Difference: Profit and Loss Account vs. Appropriation Account</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Profit and Loss Account</th><th>Profit and Loss Appropriation Account</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Calculates net profit by deducting expenses from gross profit</td><td>Shows how the already-calculated net profit is shared among partners</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Prepared by both sole traders and partnerships</td><td>Prepared only by partnerships (and companies, in a similar form)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Deals with business running expenses (rent, wages, etc.)</td><td>Deals with partners\' salaries, interest, commission, and profit shares</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Items Appearing in the Appropriation Account</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Net profit b/d</td><td>Added (credited) as the starting point of the account</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Interest on drawings</td><td>Added back to profit (credited) — a charge against partners for withdrawing cash early</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Interest on capital</td><td>Deducted (debited) — a reward paid to partners for capital invested</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Partners\' salary</td><td>Deducted (debited) — payment for a partner\'s extra work/responsibility</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Partners\' commission</td><td>Deducted (debited) — reward based on performance (e.g., % of sales)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Share of residual profit/loss</td><td>The remaining balance shared among partners per the profit-sharing ratio</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Basic Format</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Dr. side (deductions)</th><th>Cr. side (additions)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Interest on capital</td><td>Net profit b/d</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Partners\' salary</td><td>Interest on drawings</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Partners\' commission</td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Share of profit (each partner)</td><td></td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: Interest ON capital is an expense to the business (deducted); Interest ON drawings is income to the business (added back). Many students confuse these two — the word "on" doesn\'t tell you the direction, the logic does!\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The Profit and Loss Appropriation Account shows how a partnership\'s net profit is shared, accounting for interest on capital, interest on drawings, partners\' salaries, commission, before splitting the residual profit according to the agreed ratio.\n</div>\n  ',
