    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">Partnership Capital Account</span> records each partner\'s investment in the business — the funds and assets they have permanently contributed to establish and grow the partnership.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Definition</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The Capital Account shows the amount of capital each partner has contributed to the business. Unlike a current account (which fluctuates with day-to-day transactions like drawings and profit shares), a capital account under the fixed method generally remains stable unless a partner introduces additional capital or permanently withdraws part of it.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Features of Capital Accounts</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Capital accounts typically record: the original capital contributed at the start of the partnership, any additional capital introduced during the year, and any permanent withdrawal of capital. They do NOT usually record routine drawings, interest on capital/drawings, salary, or profit share — these go in the Current Account instead (under the fixed capital method).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Fixed vs. Fluctuating Capital Accounts</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Fixed Capital Method</th><th>Fluctuating Capital Method</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Capital account balance remains constant year to year</td><td>Capital account balance changes every year</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Separate Current Account records drawings, interest, salary, profit share</td><td>All items (drawings, interest, salary, profit share) are recorded directly in the Capital Account</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Requires two accounts per partner (Capital + Current)</td><td>Requires only one account per partner (Capital only)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>More commonly used in practice, as it\'s clearer for tracking permanent investment</td><td>Simpler but can make tracking permanent capital harder</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Format of the Fixed Capital Account</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Dr. side</th><th>Cr. side</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Capital withdrawn (permanent)</td><td>Balance b/d (opening capital)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Balance c/d (closing capital)</td><td>Additional capital introduced</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The clearest exam distinction: under the FIXED method, the Capital Account balance rarely changes — routine items (drawings, interest, salary, profit share) go into the Current Account instead. Under the FLUCTUATING method, everything goes into one single Capital Account.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The Partnership Capital Account tracks each partner\'s permanent investment in the business. Under the fixed capital method, it stays stable while a separate Current Account absorbs routine items; under the fluctuating method, all items are combined in a single account.\n</div>\n  ',
