    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">Partnership Current Account</span> records the day-to-day fluctuating transactions between a partner and the business, keeping the more stable Capital Account separate under the fixed capital method.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The Current Account tracks amounts a partner is entitled to (like salary, interest on capital, and profit share) against amounts they have taken out (like drawings and interest charged on those drawings). Its balance can be in credit (business owes the partner) or debit (partner owes the business).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Purpose</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The Current Account allows a partnership to keep the Capital Account stable and focused purely on permanent investment, while still accurately tracking the constantly changing balance of what each partner is owed or owes based on ongoing business activity.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Items Affecting the Current Account</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Side (in the Current Account)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Balance b/d (if credit)</td><td>Credit side</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Interest on capital</td><td>Credit side (partner is owed this)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Partners\' salary</td><td>Credit side (partner is owed this)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Share of profit</td><td>Credit side (partner is owed this)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Drawings</td><td>Debit side (reduces what\'s owed to partner)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Interest on drawings</td><td>Debit side (charged against the partner)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Format of the Current Account</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Dr. side</th><th>Cr. side</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Drawings</td><td>Balance b/d</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Interest on drawings</td><td>Interest on capital</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Balance c/d (if in credit overall)</td><td>Salary</td></tr>\n      <tr><td></td><td>Share of profit</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> If a partner\'s total drawings and charges exceed what they\'re entitled to (salary + interest + profit share), their Current Account will show a DEBIT balance — meaning the partner actually owes the business money.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The Current Account records what each partner is owed (interest on capital, salary, profit share) against what they\'ve drawn out (drawings, interest on drawings), keeping the Capital Account stable and reserved purely for permanent investment.\n</div>\n  ',
