    topic: 'Admission of a New Partner and Goodwill Account',
    topicCode: 'SS2-ACC-05',
    module: 'Partnership Accounts',
    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  When a new partner joins an existing partnership, the business\'s <span class="learn-keyword">goodwill</span> — the value of its established reputation — must be accounted for fairly, along with any changes to asset and liability values.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Goodwill</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Goodwill is the extra value a business has beyond its identifiable net assets, arising from factors like reputation, customer loyalty, location, and staff expertise built up over time. When a new partner is admitted, they benefit from this existing goodwill, so they must compensate the old partners for their share of it.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Goodwill</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Purchased goodwill</td><td>Goodwill that has been paid for, e.g., when buying an existing business</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Inherent (non-purchased) goodwill</td><td>Goodwill built up internally over time through good reputation and performance, not directly paid for</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Methods of Valuing Goodwill</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Common methods include: average profits method (goodwill = average profit × agreed number of years\' purchase), super profits method (goodwill based on profit above a normal expected return), and capitalization method (goodwill = capitalized value of average profits minus net assets).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Treatment of Goodwill on Admission of a New Partner</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">When a new partner is admitted, existing partners\' profit-sharing ratios change, meaning old partners effectively give up a portion of future profits to the new partner. The new partner should compensate the old partners for this loss, either by: (a) paying a goodwill amount directly to old partners (outside the business), or (b) bringing in capital that includes a premium for goodwill, credited to the old partners in their old profit-sharing ratio (or sacrificing ratio).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Revaluation of Assets and Liabilities</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Before a new partner is admitted, assets and liabilities are often revalued to reflect current fair value. Any resulting profit or loss on revaluation is shared among the OLD partners only (in their old ratio), since it relates to value created or lost before the new partner joined.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: revaluation profit/loss belongs to the OLD partners only (old ratio), while goodwill compensation is paid BY the new partner TO the old partners (in their sacrificing ratio) — these are two distinct concepts often tested together.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Goodwill represents the value of a business\'s reputation beyond its net assets. When a new partner is admitted, they must compensate old partners for the goodwill they benefit from, while any asset/liability revaluation gains or losses are shared only among the old partners in their old ratio.\n</div>\n  ',
