    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Many small businesses do not keep full double-entry records. <span class="learn-keyword">Single entry (incomplete records)</span> accounting techniques allow accountants to still prepare meaningful final accounts from these partial records.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Single Entry</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Single entry (or incomplete records) refers to a bookkeeping system where transactions are not recorded following the full double-entry principle (debit and credit for every transaction). Instead, records might only include a cash book, personal accounts of debtors/creditors, and few or no other ledger accounts.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Limitations of Single Entry</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Because it lacks the checks and balances of double-entry (like the trial balance), single entry makes it harder to verify accuracy, detect fraud or errors, and prepare complete financial statements directly. Additional analysis and estimation are needed to reconstruct missing figures.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Statement of Affairs Method</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Since a business using single entry has no opening capital figure readily available, accountants prepare a Statement of Affairs (similar to a balance sheet) at the start and end of the period, listing all known assets and liabilities. The capital figure is calculated as: <strong>Capital = Total Assets − Total Liabilities</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Calculating Profit from Incomplete Records</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>1. Find opening capital</td><td>Opening Total Assets − Opening Total Liabilities</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>2. Find closing capital</td><td>Closing Total Assets − Closing Total Liabilities</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>3. Find profit</td><td>Closing Capital − Opening Capital + Drawings − Additional Capital Introduced</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Converting Single Entry to Double Entry / Final Accounts</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">To prepare full final accounts (Trading, Profit and Loss Account, and Balance Sheet) from incomplete records, accountants must reconstruct missing figures — often using control accounts (e.g., Total Debtors Account, Total Creditors Account) to calculate figures like credit sales or credit purchases based on known opening/closing balances and cash movements.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Memorize the profit formula: Profit = (Closing Capital − Opening Capital) + Drawings − Additional Capital Introduced. This is one of the most frequently tested formulas in incomplete records questions.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Single entry (incomplete records) accounting lacks full double-entry bookkeeping, requiring the Statement of Affairs method to determine capital, and specific formulas and control accounts to reconstruct profit and other missing figures for final accounts preparation.\n</div>\n  ',
