    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Forming a <span class="learn-keyword">limited liability company</span> is a formal legal process that transforms a business idea into a separate legal entity, distinct from its owners, involving specific stages and documentation.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of a Company</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A company is a legal entity formed by a group of people (shareholders) to carry on a business, recognized by law as having its own separate legal identity, distinct from its owners. This means the company can own property, sue and be sued, and continue existing even if ownership changes.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Stages in the Formation of a Company</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Promotion</td><td>Initial planning stage — promoters identify the business idea, arrange initial capital, and prepare necessary documents</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Incorporation</td><td>The legal process of registering the company with the appropriate government body (e.g., Corporate Affairs Commission in Nigeria), resulting in a Certificate of Incorporation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Flotation (Capital subscription)</td><td>The company raises the capital it needs by issuing shares to investors, either privately or publicly</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Key Documents Required</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Memorandum of Association</td><td>Defines the company\'s relationship with the outside world — its name, objectives, and scope of activities</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Articles of Association</td><td>Sets out the internal rules and regulations governing how the company will be managed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Prospectus</td><td>An invitation to the public to buy shares, providing detailed information about the company (used mainly by public companies)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Certificate of Incorporation</td><td>The official legal document confirming the company has been legally registered and now exists as a separate legal entity</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the order: Promotion → Incorporation → Flotation. Also distinguish the Memorandum (external relationship) from the Articles (internal rules) — a very common exam distinction.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Forming a company involves promotion, incorporation, and flotation stages, requiring key documents like the Memorandum of Association, Articles of Association, Prospectus, and ultimately a Certificate of Incorporation to legally establish the company as a separate legal entity.\n</div>\n  ',
