    topic: 'Private and Public Companies',
    topicCode: 'SS2-ACC-09',
    module: 'Company Accounts',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Companies can be classified as either <span class="learn-keyword">private</span> or <span class="learn-keyword">public</span>, based on how they raise capital, how their shares can be transferred, and the regulatory requirements they must follow.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Private and Public Companies</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A private company is a company whose shares are NOT offered to the general public and are typically restricted in how they can be transferred, often owned by a small group (like family or close associates). A public company is a company whose shares CAN be offered to and traded by the general public, often listed on a stock exchange, with generally larger capital requirements.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Key Features Compared</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Private Company</th><th>Public Company</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Minimum members</td><td>Usually 2 (varies by country)</td><td>Usually 2, sometimes 7 (varies by country)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Maximum members</td><td>Often limited (e.g., 50 in many jurisdictions)</td><td>No maximum limit</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sale of shares to public</td><td>Not permitted</td><td>Permitted, often via a Prospectus</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Transfer of shares</td><td>Restricted</td><td>Freely transferable</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Name suffix (Nigeria)</td><td>"Limited" (Ltd)</td><td>"Public Limited Company" (Plc)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Advantages and Disadvantages of Private Companies</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Advantages:</strong> greater privacy (less public disclosure required), more control retained by founders/family, simpler and cheaper to set up and run. <strong>Disadvantages:</strong> limited access to capital (cannot sell shares to the public), restricted growth potential, harder to transfer ownership.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Advantages and Disadvantages of Public Companies</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Advantages:</strong> access to large amounts of capital from the public, shares easily bought/sold (liquidity), enhanced company reputation/credibility. <strong>Disadvantages:</strong> more stringent regulatory and disclosure requirements, higher cost of compliance, risk of loss of control (as ownership becomes widely spread) or hostile takeover.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the naming convention as a quick identifier: "Ltd" = Private Company; "Plc" = Public Limited Company. This is a frequently tested, easy-to-remember distinction.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Private companies restrict share transfer and public sale, offering privacy and control but limited capital access; public companies can sell shares to the public and are freely transferable, offering greater capital access but facing stricter regulation and less concentrated control.\n</div>\n  ',
