    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Issuing <span class="learn-keyword">shares</span> is the primary way companies raise long-term capital from investors, who in turn become part-owners of the company with specific rights depending on the type of share held.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Shares</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A share represents a unit of ownership in a company. When an investor buys shares, they become a shareholder — a part-owner of the company — entitled to a portion of profits (dividends) and, in most cases, a vote in major company decisions.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Shares</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Key Features</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Ordinary shares</td><td>Carry voting rights; dividend is variable and paid only after other obligations are met; higher risk, higher potential reward</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Preference shares</td><td>Fixed dividend rate; paid before ordinary shareholders; usually no voting rights; lower risk than ordinary shares</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Deferred shares</td><td>Dividend paid only after both preference and ordinary shareholders have been paid; often held by founders; higher risk</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Methods of Issuing Shares</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Public offer</td><td>Shares offered directly to the general public for subscription, usually via a Prospectus</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Rights issue</td><td>New shares offered first to EXISTING shareholders, usually at a discounted price, in proportion to their current holding</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bonus issue</td><td>Free additional shares given to existing shareholders (funded from company reserves), with no new cash raised</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Basic Accounting Entries for Share Issue</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">When shares are issued at par (nominal value), the entry is: Debit Bank/Cash, Credit Share Capital. If shares are issued at a premium (above nominal value), the excess is credited to a separate Share Premium Account: Debit Bank/Cash (full amount received), Credit Share Capital (nominal value), Credit Share Premium (excess above nominal value).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the risk/reward order (from lowest to highest risk for dividend priority): Preference shares → Ordinary shares → Deferred shares. Preference shareholders are paid first, deferred shareholders last.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Companies raise capital by issuing ordinary, preference, or deferred shares through public offers, rights issues, or bonus issues. Shares issued above nominal value generate a share premium, recorded separately from ordinary share capital.\n</div>\n  ',
