    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Beyond issuing shares, companies can raise funds through <span class="learn-keyword">loan capital</span> — borrowing money that must be repaid with interest, rather than giving up ownership stakes in the business.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Loan Capital</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Loan capital refers to funds a company borrows (rather than raises through share capital), which must be repaid at a future date along with interest. Unlike shareholders, lenders (creditors) do not become owners of the company and typically have no voting rights, but they are entitled to receive interest payments regardless of whether the company makes a profit.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Loan Capital</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Debentures</td><td>A formal, long-term debt instrument issued by a company, often secured against specific assets, paying fixed interest</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bonds</td><td>Similar to debentures — long-term debt securities that pay periodic interest and repay the principal at maturity</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bank loans</td><td>Direct borrowing from a bank, usually with a fixed repayment schedule and specific terms/collateral requirements</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Advantages and Disadvantages of Loan Capital</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Advantages:</strong> does not dilute ownership/control (unlike issuing new shares), interest paid is usually tax-deductible (reducing taxable profit), can be a cheaper source of finance than equity in some cases. <strong>Disadvantages:</strong> interest must be paid regardless of profitability, increases financial risk (especially in poor trading periods), may require collateral/security, and excessive borrowing can lead to insolvency if the company cannot meet repayment obligations.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Accounting Treatment of Loan Capital</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">When a loan/debenture is issued: Debit Bank/Cash, Credit Debentures/Loan Account (a long-term liability). Interest paid on the loan is treated as an expense in the Profit and Loss Account (Debit Interest Expense, Credit Bank), unlike dividends, which are an appropriation of profit, not an expense.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Key distinction: Interest on loan capital is a business EXPENSE (deducted before profit is calculated); dividends on share capital are an APPROPRIATION of profit (paid after profit is calculated). This affects where each appears in the financial statements.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Loan capital (debentures, bonds, bank loans) allows companies to raise funds without diluting ownership, requiring fixed interest payments regardless of profitability. Unlike dividends, loan interest is treated as a business expense, not a profit appropriation.\n</div>\n  ',
