    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Companies and governments can access the <span class="learn-keyword">capital market</span> through several distinct methods, each with its own advantages, costs, and suitability depending on the issuer\'s circumstances.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Public Issue</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A public issue involves inviting members of the general public to subscribe for shares or bonds directly, usually through a Prospectus. This method can raise large amounts of capital but is costly and time-consuming due to extensive regulatory and marketing requirements.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Rights Issue</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A rights issue offers new shares to existing shareholders in proportion to their current holdings, usually at a discount to the market price. This is generally cheaper and faster than a public issue since it targets an already-known group of investors.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Private Placement</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A private placement involves selling shares or bonds directly to a select group of investors (e.g., institutional investors like pension funds or insurance companies) rather than the general public. This method is faster and less costly than a public issue but reaches a smaller pool of potential investors.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Offer for Sale</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">An offer for sale involves a company selling its shares to an issuing house (a financial intermediary), which then resells these shares to the public at a markup. This shifts much of the administrative burden to the issuing house.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Government Bonds</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Governments raise funds from the capital market by issuing bonds, promising to repay the principal with interest at a future date. This allows governments to finance infrastructure and other projects without immediately raising taxes.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Key Advantage</th><th>Key Disadvantage</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Public issue</td><td>Can raise very large sums</td><td>Costly, time-consuming, heavily regulated</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Rights issue</td><td>Cheaper, protects existing shareholders</td><td>May raise less capital than a public issue</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Private placement</td><td>Fast, less costly, less regulatory burden</td><td>Limited pool of investors, less public visibility</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Offer for sale</td><td>Administrative burden shifted to issuing house</td><td>Company may receive a lower net price for shares</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Government bonds</td><td>Funds public projects without immediate tax increases</td><td>Creates future repayment/interest obligations for government</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to match a scenario to the appropriate method — e.g., "a company wants to raise funds quickly from a few large institutional investors" points clearly to private placement.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Companies and governments raise funds from the capital market through public issues, rights issues, private placements, offers for sale, and government bonds, each offering distinct trade-offs in cost, speed, regulatory burden, and the amount of capital that can be raised.\n</div>\n  ',
