    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Final accounts</span> are the set of financial statements prepared at the end of an accounting period to show a business\'s trading results and overall financial position.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Components of Final Accounts</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Trading Account</td><td>Calculates gross profit (Sales − Cost of Goods Sold)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Profit and Loss Account</td><td>Calculates net profit (Gross profit − Operating expenses + Other income)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Balance Sheet</td><td>Shows the business\'s financial position — assets, liabilities, and capital — at a specific date</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Year-End Adjustments</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Before preparing final accounts, several adjustments are usually necessary to ensure figures reflect the correct accounting period (matching/accrual concept): closing stock valuation, accrued expenses (owed but not yet paid), prepaid expenses (paid in advance), accrued income (earned but not yet received), depreciation of fixed assets, and provision for doubtful debts / bad debts written off.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Common Adjustment Treatments</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Adjustment</th><th>Effect on Accounts</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Accrued expense</td><td>Added to the expense in P&L; shown as a current liability in the Balance Sheet</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Prepaid expense</td><td>Deducted from the expense in P&L; shown as a current asset in the Balance Sheet</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Depreciation</td><td>Charged as an expense in P&L; reduces the asset\'s book value in the Balance Sheet</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bad debts</td><td>Charged as an expense in P&L; deducted from debtors in the Balance Sheet</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Provision for doubtful debts</td><td>Charged/adjusted as an expense in P&L; shown as a deduction from debtors in the Balance Sheet</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. The Balance Sheet Equation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The Balance Sheet must always balance according to the fundamental accounting equation: <strong>Assets = Liabilities + Capital</strong>. Every adjustment made in preparing final accounts must be correctly reflected on both sides to maintain this balance.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Every adjustment affects TWO places in the final accounts (e.g., accrued expense appears both in the P&L as an added expense AND in the Balance Sheet as a current liability) — always find both effects to avoid losing marks.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Final accounts (Trading, Profit and Loss, and Balance Sheet) show a business\'s trading results and financial position, requiring careful year-end adjustments for accruals, prepayments, depreciation, and bad/doubtful debts to ensure figures are accurately matched to the correct accounting period.\n</div>\n  ',
