    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Effective <span class="learn-keyword">regulation</span> of the capital market is essential to protect investors, ensure fair dealing, and maintain overall confidence in the financial system.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Capital Market Regulation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Capital market regulation refers to the rules, laws, and oversight mechanisms established by government bodies to govern how the capital market operates — covering everything from company listings to trading practices, investor protection, and disclosure requirements.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Key Regulatory Bodies in Nigeria</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Body</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)</td><td>The apex regulator of Nigeria\'s capital market, overseeing market operators, approving new issues, and protecting investors</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nigerian Exchange Group (NGX)</td><td>Operates the stock exchange itself, setting listing rules and facilitating trading of listed securities</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN)</td><td>Regulates the broader financial system, including banks that operate within the capital market</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Corporate Affairs Commission (CAC)</td><td>Registers companies, a foundational step before capital market participation</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Objectives of Capital Market Regulation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Regulation aims to protect investors from fraud and unfair practices, ensure market transparency through mandatory disclosure, maintain market stability and integrity, prevent insider trading and market manipulation, and promote fair competition among market participants.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Methods of Regulation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Regulators use methods such as licensing of market operators (stockbrokers, issuing houses), mandatory disclosure requirements (Prospectus, periodic financial reporting), enforcement actions against violators (fines, suspension, delisting), and setting rules for trading conduct and market operations.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Know the difference: SEC is the overall market regulator (like a referee), while NGX operates the exchange itself (like the venue/pitch where trading happens) — a common area of confusion in exams.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Capital market regulation, led in Nigeria by the SEC (with NGX operating the exchange itself), protects investors, ensures transparency, maintains market stability, and prevents fraud and manipulation through licensing, disclosure requirements, and enforcement actions.\n</div>\n  ',
