    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Hire purchase</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">instalment payment</span> arrangements allow buyers to acquire and use an asset while paying for it gradually over time, each with distinct legal and accounting treatment.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Hire Purchase</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Hire purchase is an arrangement where the buyer (hirer) takes possession of an asset and pays for it in instalments, but LEGAL OWNERSHIP only transfers to the buyer after the FINAL instalment is paid. Until then, the seller (hire vendor) remains the legal owner, and can repossess the asset if payments are not maintained.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Meaning of Instalment Payment (Credit Sale)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Under an instalment payment (credit sale) arrangement, ownership of the asset transfers to the buyer IMMEDIATELY at the point of sale, even though payment is still spread over instalments. The buyer becomes the legal owner right away and bears full risk/responsibility for the asset from that point.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Key Differences</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Hire Purchase</th><th>Instalment Payment (Credit Sale)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Ownership transfer</td><td>Only after final instalment is paid</td><td>Immediately at the point of sale</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Right to repossess if default occurs</td><td>Seller can repossess the asset</td><td>Seller cannot repossess (must sue for the debt instead)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Buyer\'s status before final payment</td><td>Hirer (not yet owner)</td><td>Owner (from the start)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Components of Hire Purchase Price</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The total Hire Purchase Price consists of: the Cash Price (what the asset would cost if bought outright) plus Hire Purchase Interest (the extra charge for spreading payment over time). Interest is usually calculated and apportioned across the instalment periods, often using methods like the sum-of-digits (Rule of 78) method to allocate more interest to earlier periods.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Basic Accounting Entries</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The buyer records the asset at its cash price (Debit Asset Account), with the Hire Purchase Interest treated as a separate expense charged to the Profit and Loss Account over the payment period, while the outstanding hire purchase liability is shown in the Balance Sheet.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the critical legal distinction: under Hire Purchase, ownership transfers ONLY after the FINAL payment (seller can repossess before then); under Instalment Payment/Credit Sale, ownership transfers IMMEDIATELY (seller can only sue for unpaid debt, not repossess).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Hire purchase delays legal ownership transfer until the final instalment is paid (allowing repossession on default), while instalment payment/credit sale transfers ownership immediately. The Hire Purchase Price comprises the Cash Price plus Hire Purchase Interest, apportioned across the payment period.\n</div>\n  ',
